WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.748
Deserialization converts an external
representation into

2
00:00:02.748 --> 00:00:04.809
values the application can inspect.

3
00:00:04.809 --> 00:00:08.369
In TypeScript services, that boundary
includes JSON request bodies,

4
00:00:08.369 --> 00:00:12.116
decoded queue messages, configuration
files, webhooks, imported records, cache

5
00:00:12.116 --> 00:00:15.769
entries, and database JSON written by less
trusted producers.

6
00:00:15.769 --> 00:00:19.703
A successful conversion tells you that the
representation was readable.

7
00:00:19.703 --> 00:00:22.701
It does not establish that the resulting
value

8
00:00:22.701 --> 00:00:25.511
is an acceptable request for your
application.

9
00:00:25.511 --> 00:00:29.071
JSON parsing recognizes syntax and
produces ordinary JavaScript values.

10
00:00:29.071 --> 00:00:34.035
The result may be an object, an array, a
primitive, or null.

11
00:00:34.035 --> 00:00:37.127
Parsing does not require an account
identifier, bound

12
00:00:37.127 --> 00:00:39.937
a quantity, reject an unexpected nested
field,

13
00:00:39.937 --> 00:00:43.122
or establish which operation the sender
may perform.

14
00:00:43.122 --> 00:00:45.745
Assigning the parsed result an account
update

15
00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:48.555
type merely changes what the compiler
assumes.

16
00:00:48.555 --> 00:00:50.803
Passing that value directly to an

17
00:00:50.803 --> 00:00:53.613
update function leaves the runtime
boundary unverified.

18
00:00:53.613 --> 00:00:57.173
Treat the boundary as a sequence of
explicit decisions.

19
00:00:57.173 --> 00:00:59.234
Limit incoming bytes before parsing.

20
00:00:59.234 --> 00:01:01.669
Select the intended format and parser.

21
00:01:01.669 --> 00:01:04.854
Validate the resulting value against a
runtime schema.

22
00:01:04.854 --> 00:01:07.571
Check domain rules using appropriate
trusted context,

23
00:01:07.571 --> 00:01:10.662
map accepted fields into a narrow
application value,

24
00:01:10.662 --> 00:01:13.472
and enforce authorization before any
protected effect.

25
00:01:13.472 --> 00:01:17.875
Some checks depend on one another, so
their precise placement varies.

26
00:01:17.875 --> 00:01:20.498
Each responsibility still needs an
identifiable owner

27
00:01:20.498 --> 00:01:23.308
and a rejection path that stops
processing.

28
00:01:23.308 --> 00:01:25.743
Authentication does not remove these
responsibilities.

29
00:01:25.743 --> 00:01:29.303
A signed webhook can carry an unsupported
message version.

30
00:01:29.303 --> 00:01:32.301
An authenticated user can submit the wrong
type

31
00:01:32.301 --> 00:01:35.111
or request an operation outside their
permissions.

32
00:01:35.111 --> 00:01:39.045
Stored data can outlive the schema that
originally accepted it.

33
00:01:39.045 --> 00:01:42.511
At every entry point, identify who can
influence the

34
00:01:42.511 --> 00:01:46.071
representation and what evidence the
consuming code actually receives.

35
00:01:46.071 --> 00:01:49.162
Reject malformed or unacceptable input
with controlled errors,

36
00:01:49.162 --> 00:01:52.347
without echoing sensitive payloads or
allowing partial writes.

37
00:01:52.347 --> 00:01:54.970
The useful outcome of deserialization is a

38
00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:57.780
constrained value whose permitted uses are
clear.

